      "hints": [
        "Find $f^{-1}(x) = \\frac{4 - x}{5}$.",
        "Set $f(x) = x \\implies 4 - 5x = x \\implies x = \\frac{2}{3}$.",
        "Intersection point is $\\left(\\frac{2}{3}, \\frac{2}{3}\\right)$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Find the inverse function $f^{-1}(x)$ by setting $y = 4 - 5x$ and rearranging for $x$:\n$$5x = 4 - y \\implies x = \\frac{4 - y}{5} \\implies f^{-1}(x) = \\frac{4 - x}{5}$$",
        "Find the point of intersection by setting $f(x) = f^{-1}(x)$, which is equivalent to solving $f(x) = x$ on the line of symmetry $y = x$:\n$$4 - 5x = x \\implies 6x = 4 \\implies x = \\frac{2}{3}$$\n$$y = 4 - 5\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) = 4 - \\frac{10}{3} = \\frac{2}{3}$$\n$$\\text{Point of intersection: } \\left(\\frac{2}{3}, \\frac{2}{3}\\right)$$"
      ],
